title: Killing the pacer does not end the session it started, so restart it by 'stop' and a waiter, never by 'kill'.
pid: e523b4c9-e2d4-4560-81cb-3d89de1286c8
body: |2-
  A session runs in a subshell of the pacer under 'timeout', which puts
  itself and the session in a process group of their own. So killing
  the pacer, or even its process group, leaves the session running,
  unwatched: no "ended" line, no check of Jeff's files, no account row.
  'stop' has the pacer exit a minute after its session ends; a waiter
  on its pid then starts the new one, as Top's root note says. Since
  2026-11-15 a pacer started too soon refuses, so the waiter loses nothing.
